Ours (amigaux).  In a release build gcc's __builtin_unreachable() lets gcc drop
the range check in front of a switch whose default is Py_UNREACHABLE(), and
the 060 gate's scanner then cannot size the jump table (11 such dispatch sites
in python3.11).  On Amix, Py_UNREACHABLE() is Py_FatalError(), as in a debug
build: one compare per switch, and the table is bounded in the code.

--- a/Include/pymacro.h
+++ b/Include/pymacro.h
@@ -140,6 +140,10 @@
         "We've reached an unreachable state. Anything is possible.\n" \
         "The limits were in our heads all along. Follow your dreams.\n" \
         "https://xkcd.com/2200")
+#elif defined(__AMIX__)
+/* Amix: keep each switch's range check, so the 060 gate can size its table */
+#  define Py_UNREACHABLE() \
+    Py_FatalError("Unreachable C code path reached")
 #elif defined(__GNUC__) && (__GNUC__ > 4 || (__GNUC__ == 4 && __GNUC_MINOR__ >= 5))
 #  define Py_UNREACHABLE() __builtin_unreachable()
 #elif defined(__clang__) || defined(__INTEL_COMPILER)
